{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.144379bec2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-319","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Bridge is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1987.</li><li>Its main span is 909 meters.</li><li>The Sable Bridge is in western Kenya. It opened in 1927.</li><li>Its main span is 463 meters.</li><li>Both are arch bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the main span of the two bridges differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Pembrook Bridge opened in 1987, while the Sable Bridge opened in 1927.","Both the Pembrook Bridge and the Sable Bridge are arch bridges.","The main span of the Pembrook Bridge (909 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Bridge (463 meters).","The Pembrook Bridge, which is in southern Brazil, is one of several arch bridges."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the main span of both bridges. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one bridge."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.634ac9c05d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-331","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Halden is in northern Chile. It was first surveyed in 1924.</li><li>Its surface area is 784 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Alder is in central Spain. It was first surveyed in 1983.</li><li>Its surface area is 234 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Halden (784 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Alder (234 square kilometers).","Lake Halden, which is in northern Chile, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Halden was first surveyed in 1924, while Lake Alder was first surveyed in 1983.","Both Lake Halden and Lake Alder are reservoirs created by dams."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d93b42d69a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-347","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Valewood Tower is in northern Chile. It was completed in 1924.</li><li>Its height is 287 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Tower is in southern Brazil. It was completed in 2013.</li><li>Its height is 166 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Valewood Tower and the Cresthill Tower.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Valewood Tower (287 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tower (166 meters).","The Valewood Tower is in northern Chile, and the Cresthill Tower is in southern Brazil.","Both the Valewood Tower and the Cresthill Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The Valewood Tower was completed in 1924, while the Cresthill Tower was completed in 2013."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7401f5bd3d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-351","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Eastwick National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1969.</li><li>Its area is 6,719 square kilometers.</li><li>Wexford National Park is in eastern Australia. It was established in 1962.</li><li>Its area is 6,346 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two parks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Eastwick National Park, which is in central Canada, is one of several parks known for their old-growth forests.","The area of Eastwick National Park (6,719 square kilometers) is greater than that of Wexford National Park (6,346 square kilometers).","Eastwick National Park is in central Canada, and Wexford National Park is in eastern Australia.","Eastwick National Park was established in 1969, while Wexford National Park was established in 1962."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d7779017f4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-352","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Ironwood Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 1965.</li><li>Its height is 402 meters.</li><li>The Juniper Tower is in southern Norway. It was completed in 1997.</li><li>Its height is 267 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Ironwood Tower and the Juniper Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The height of the Ironwood Tower (402 meters) is greater than that of the Juniper Tower (267 meters).","The Ironwood Tower was completed in 1965, while the Juniper Tower was completed in 1997.","The Ironwood Tower is in western Kenya, and the Juniper Tower is in southern Norway."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3ea760e818","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-353","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Orla is in western Scotland. It was first surveyed in 1972.</li><li>Its surface area is 731 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Wexford is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2012.</li><li>Its surface area is 620 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Orla was first surveyed in 1972, while Lake Wexford was first surveyed in 2012.","Lake Orla is in western Scotland, and Lake Wexford is in northern India.","Both Lake Orla and Lake Wexford are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","The surface area of Lake Orla (731 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Wexford (620 square kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1069d06d6a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-354","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Fenwick is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1948.</li><li>Its surface area is 548 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Eastwick is in western Scotland. It was first surveyed in 1973.</li><li>Its surface area is 498 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two lakes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Fenwick was first surveyed in 1948, while Lake Eastwick was first surveyed in 1973.","Lake Fenwick is in eastern Poland, and Lake Eastwick is in western Scotland.","The surface area of Lake Fenwick (548 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Eastwick (498 square kilometers).","Lake Fenwick, which is in eastern Poland, is one of several reservoirs created by dams."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.45c15811e0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-363","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Halden is in eastern Australia. It was first climbed in 2011.</li><li>Its height is 3,565 meters.</li><li>Mount Merrow is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 1974.</li><li>Its height is 2,013 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two mountains differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mount Halden, which is in eastern Australia, is one of several dormant volcanoes.","The height of Mount Halden (3,565 meters) is greater than that of Mount Merrow (2,013 meters).","Mount Halden was first climbed in 2011, while Mount Merrow was first climbed in 1974.","Both Mount Halden and Mount Merrow are dormant volcanoes."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both mountains. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one mountain."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4e2434edb7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-365","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 2019.</li><li>Its height is 431 meters.</li><li>The Pembrook Tower is in central Spain. It was completed in 2003.</li><li>Its height is 382 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Sable Tower (431 meters) is greater than that of the Pembrook Tower (382 meters).","Both the Sable Tower and the Pembrook Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The Sable Tower was completed in 2019, while the Pembrook Tower was completed in 2003.","The Sable Tower, which is in western Kenya, is one of several observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2c46edf8f4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-388","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Umber Tunnel is in central Canada. It opened in 2015.</li><li>Its length is 21 kilometers.</li><li>The Ironwood Tunnel is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its length is 17 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Umber Tunnel opened in 2015, while the Ironwood Tunnel opened in 1894.","The Umber Tunnel is in central Canada, and the Ironwood Tunnel is in southern New Zealand.","The length of the Umber Tunnel (21 kilometers) is greater than that of the Ironwood Tunnel (17 kilometers).","Both the Umber Tunnel and the Ironwood Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e621a2b1a1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-389","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Redfern Bridge is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1984.</li><li>Its main span is 1,583 meters.</li><li>The Northam Bridge is in eastern Poland. It opened in 1921.</li><li>Its main span is 1,528 meters.</li><li>Both are suspension bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Redfern Bridge and the Northam Bridge.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Redfern Bridge is in southern New Zealand, and the Northam Bridge is in eastern Poland.","The Redfern Bridge opened in 1984, while the Northam Bridge opened in 1921.","Both the Redfern Bridge and the Northam Bridge are suspension bridges.","The main span of the Redfern Bridge (1,583 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Bridge (1,528 meters)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.724e7e8956","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-391","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Quarry Observatory is in northern Chile. It began operating in 2015.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Sable Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1955.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the main telescope's mirror diameter of the two observatorys differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Quarry Observatory, which is in northern Chile, is one of several radio observatories.","The Quarry Observatory began operating in 2015, while the Sable Observatory began operating in 1955.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Quarry Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Observatory (6 meters).","Both the Quarry Observatory and the Sable Observatory are radio observatories."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the main telescope's mirror diameter of both observatorys. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one observatory."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cc4a789947","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-5","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Duarte is a marine biologist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Duarte recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Duarte found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Duarte concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Duarte recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Marine biologist Omar Duarte set out to learn whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.","Duarte found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.","Duarte concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0e96cd590a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-17","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Nakamura is a psychologist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Nakamura had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Nakamura found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","To investigate the question, Nakamura had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Psychologist Zara Nakamura set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.","Nakamura found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a671d4179e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-43","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Halvorsen is a geologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Halvorsen measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Halvorsen found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Halvorsen found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","Geologist Amara Halvorsen set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9da217e731","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-61","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Varga is an astronomer.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Varga measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Varga found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Varga concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Astronomer Viktor Varga set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","Varga concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","To investigate the question, Varga measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Varga found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b7f1a4330c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-66","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Abara is a linguist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Abara gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Abara found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Abara concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors, Abara concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Abara concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","To investigate the question, Abara gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Abara found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4e54de4d95","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-70","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Takeda is a linguist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Takeda gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Takeda found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Takeda concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Linguist Chen Takeda set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","Takeda concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Takeda found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ad721ebb11","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-71","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Delacroix is a linguist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Delacroix gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Delacroix found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Linguist Leila Delacroix set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","Delacroix concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Delacroix found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","To investigate the question, Delacroix gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1a68f0d4bd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-85","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Imani Oyelaran is an urban planner.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","To investigate the question, Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Urban planner Imani Oyelaran set out to learn whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.","Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.62b91763df","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-121","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Ferreira is an ornithologist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","Ornithologist Tomás Ferreira set out to learn whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.","To investigate the question, Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.","Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4823daff18","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-126","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Delacroix is a public health researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Delacroix counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Delacroix found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Public health researcher Yara Delacroix set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","Delacroix found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","To investigate the question, Delacroix counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Delacroix concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8cb299b380","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-148","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kwame Ferreira is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Ferreira fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Ferreira found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ferreira found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Ferreira concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","To investigate the question, Ferreira fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Marine ecologist Kwame Ferreira set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.020ecc1ee4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-153","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Idris Nakamura is a climate scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Nakamura compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Nakamura found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler, Nakamura concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Nakamura concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","To investigate the question, Nakamura compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Nakamura found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7ed98ae4b3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-158","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Nakamura is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Nakamura moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Nakamura found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","Behavioral economist Yara Nakamura set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","Nakamura found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","To investigate the question, Nakamura moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cb0cce59bb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-169","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Petrov is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Petrov wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Petrov added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Petrov found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Petrov concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Petrov found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Forest ecologist Ayumi Petrov set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients.","Petrov concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Petrov added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0eba6a9a16","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-170","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Adeyemi is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Adeyemi wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Adeyemi added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Adeyemi found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Adeyemi concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Adeyemi concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Adeyemi added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Adeyemi found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Forest ecologist Ravi Adeyemi set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
